In article <2dgsltINNmud@myall.awadi.com.au>, tmason@ooldea.awadi (Bionic Tapeworm) writes:
>Peter Snelling writes:
>>My question is when will 3.2 come out, and what will be in it?

>>I'll mention a couple possibilities. Not really major new features, but 
>>more major than just a new item.  Let me know what you think, and also
>>post anything you think nethack really still needs:
>>  Support for races/roles:
>>    Instead of just picking a role (ABCEHKPRSTVW), you would pick
>>    a race (Human, Elf, ... (maybe Dwarf)) and a role (ABCHKPRSTVW).
>
>Like it.  But do the demihumans (ie. Elf, Dwarf, (Hobbit?  Gnome?  Half-orc??))
>have any advantage over human?  (eg. infra(heat) vision, elves' current sleep
>resistance/searching/see invisible)

Right - Currently, elves are better in every way than humans.  There would
have to be something they don't get, or only real experts would ever play
humans.  Maybe elves get intrinsics but lousy stats, humans get better
stats, but none of the intrinsics...

>>  A variety of quests:

>That's a good idea.  But the above change is going to be enough coding,
>what with all the new Quests (eg. Elf Barbarian Quest != Dwarf Barbarian
>Quest != Dwarf Tourist Quest).  Maybe have the WoY quest, Vlad quest,
>the race/class Quest (or one of each!) as sort of separate phases of the
>major AoY quest...?

I'd thought about that.  What I'd envisioned is only one sub-quest per role.
The races would only really affect your starting stats/intrinsics.  I
really meant "quests", not the sub-quests now in nethack.  The point
is that the basic structure for the game wouldn't have to change.  Just
one little question when you start, and then some changes to mklev.c to
determine what level to create next, depending on whether you're doing
the full blown Amulet of Yendor quest, or something smaller.  This is
just for times when you only have an hour or two to play, and would 
like a chance to play a game from beginning to end.

Another Idea:

Personally, I never use the oracle when I'm playing.  I've read all the
information they could possibly give you, so why would I bother.  What
I'd considered is giving them information which even someone who has read
every line of code (no, I have not) will still want to get from them.
While they still need the kind of information you and I read in r.g.h
spoilers, maybe they could also have other game dependant information.

At first I thought about varying the way to open the Sanctuary with the
Amulet of Yendor from game to game, and forcing everyone to see the oracle 
and pay him, in order to find out how to open the stairway this game.  But
that's kind of asking for trouble, because you'd be SOL if your pet Archon 
happened to kill the oracle.  So instead I've now thought about information
like "What level of Hell is Vlad's staircase is on?".  Things that you can 
find out with an exhaustive search, but things you'd really pay to find out. 

Opinions?
